    "innovation": "This paper creates and validates an hourly MODIS surface temperature dataset for Antarctica, using it to demonstrate a widespread warm bias in ERA-Interim reanalysis and attributing the cause to an overestimation of surface turbulent heat fluxes under very stable atmospheric conditions.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA-Interim",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "widespread warm bias",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "During conditions detected as cloud free by MODIS, ERA-Interim shows a widespread warm bias in Antarctica in every season, ranging from +3 to +6\u00b0C on the plateau.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MODIS",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "land surface temperatures",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Abstract. Moderate-Resolution Imaging spectroradiometer (MODIS) land surface temperatures in Antarctica were processed in order to produce a gridded data set at 25 km resolution, spanning the period 2000-2011 at an hourly time step.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MODIS",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MOUNTED_ON",
            "target_entity_instance": "Terra",
            "target_entity_type": "Platform",
            "paragraph": "Clear-sky LST values are derived from observations of the MODIS instruments on board Terra and Aqua spacecrafts.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MODIS",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MOUNTED_ON",
            "target_entity_instance": "Aqua",
            "target_entity_type": "Platform",
            "paragraph": "Clear-sky LST values are derived from observations of the MODIS instruments on board Terra and Aqua spacecrafts.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "pyrgeometer",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "long-wave radiation",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "To assess the accuracy of MODIS Ts values, we processed upwelling and downwelling long-wave radiation observations (LWup and LWdown) provided at an hourly time step by three BSRN (Baseline Surface Radiation Network) stations (Ohmura et al., 1998): Dome C (75\u00b006\u2032S 123\u00b020\u2032 E; 3233 ma.s.l.), South Pole (90\u00b0 S 0\u00b0 E; 2835 ma.s.l.), Syowa (69\u00b0 S 39\u00b035\u2032E; 18 ma.s.l.) and four automatic weather stations (AWSs) operated by the Institute for Marine and Atmospheric research, Utrecht University (van den Broeke et al., 2004): Kohnen (AWS9) (75\u00b0 S 0\u00b040\u2032E; 2892 ma.s.l.), Plateau Station B (AWS12) (78\u00b038\u2032S 35\u00b038\u2032E; 3619ma.s.l.), Pole of Inaccessibility (AWS13) (82\u00b03\u2032 S 54\u00b034\u2032 E; 3718 m a.s.l.), and Princess Elisabeth (AWS16), the latter operated in collaboration with the Catholic University Leuven, (Gorodetskaya et al., 2013) (71\u00b057\u2032S 23\u00b021\u2032 E; 1372 ma.s.l.). Note that the BSRN Georg Von Neumayer station (70\u00b039\u2032 S 8\u00b015\u2032 W; 42 m a.s.l.) cannot be used due to its location on the Ekstr\u00f6m ice shelf where MODIS LST products are not produced. BSRN data were collected at stations with permanent staff, thereby ensuring regular cleaning of the pyrgeometers and limiting the perturbations due to riming, which is very frequent on the Antarctic Plateau.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA-Interim",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "Integrated Forecast System",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "The ERA-Interim reanalysis includes a comprehensive set of variables describing the surface and the ABL. We focused on the ERA-Interim skin temperature, hereafter ERA-i Ts, which forms the interface between the soil and the atmosphere in the Integrated Forecast System (IFS) (European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF), http://www.ecmwf.int). IFS is the meteorological model and assimilation scheme used in the ERA-Interim analysis to assimilate observations.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA-Interim/Land",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "HTESSEL",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "This temperature, hereinafter referred to as ERA-i/land Ts, was derived from a stand-alone land-surface model simulation using the land surface model HTESSEL (Hydrology Tiled ECMWF Scheme for Surface Exchanges over Land) (Balsamo et al., 2009), with meteorological forcing from ERA-Interim.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SURFEX",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "Crocus",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "Within the modeling platform SURFEX (EXTernalized land and ocean SURFace platform) (Masson et al., 2013), the Crocus snowpack model (Brun et al., 1992; Vionnet et al., 2012) was run in a stand-alone mode, using meteorological forcing data from ERA-Interim.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Crocus",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "snow surface temperatures",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "A last run was then performed from July 1999 to December 2012, in order to produce the hourly time series of snow surface temperatures used in the following, hereinafter named Crocus Ts, covering the whole Antarctic continent at 25 km resolution.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Crocus",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "bias",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The Crocus Ts bias is much better, with values ranging from \u22122 to +2\u00b0C on most of the plateau. Local maxima correspond to areas where ERA-i Ts exhibits the warmest biases, which is consistent with the fact that Crocus was forced with ERA-Interim T2m.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Crocus",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "RMSE",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Crocus Ts RMSE is remarkably low (from 3.1 to 4.4 \u00b0C) considering the errors in the hourly in situ observations and the uncertainties in the ERA-Interim forcing. Consequently, the evaluation against in situ observations is very consistent with the one made against MODIS Ts on the whole plateau, increasing the confidence in the ERA-i Ts warm bias.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SURFEX",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Louis (1979) formulation modified by Mascart et al. (1995)",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "HTESSEL uses the same parameterization of the surface exchanges as IFS (stability function from H\u00f6gstr\u00f6m (1988)), in contrast to the formulation used in SURFEX and consequently in Crocus (Louis (1979) modified by Mascart et al. (1995), including a limitation of the maximum Richardson number).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "TESSEL",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "HAS_SUBSEQUENT_VERSION",
            "target_entity_instance": "HTESSEL",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "Though HTESSEL has a better description of snow processes than the land scheme TESSEL (Tiled ECMWF Scheme for Surface Exchanges over Land) used in IFS, ERA-i/land Ts does not significantly differ from ERA-i Ts.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Integrated Forecast System",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "TESSEL",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "Though HTESSEL has a better description of snow processes than the land scheme TESSEL (Tiled ECMWF Scheme for Surface Exchanges over Land) used in IFS, ERA-i/land Ts does not significantly differ from ERA-i Ts.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
